    /** iOS `tutorBlock`'s rule for a try answered with the app's result. */
    const val TUTOR_TRY_HELD =
        "- A TRY comes with the app's result: NOT YET (a slip, or it doesn't   do the challenge yet), ONCE MORE (right, say it again so it   sticks) or DONE (move on). Say what the note says, in your own   warm words, in that one turn. Never move on, tick, call a line   done, or ask for it again unless the note says so — the checklist   on their screen follows the app, and you must match it."

    /** iOS `tutorBlock`'s lesson part while a challenge is on and the tutor
     *  judges each try itself (iOS f75c9b5c); [weakRule] is [tutorWeakRule]
     *  or "". */
    fun tutorLessonSelf(titleTarget: String, rows: String, now: Int, weakRule: String): String =
        "TODAY'S LESSON: \"" +
            titleTarget +
            "\". The goal: by the end they can say all of it properly, out loud, with no mistakes. The checklist:\n" +
            rows +
            "\nYou are on challenge " +
            (now + 1) +
            ". YOU judge each try, the moment they finish, and answer it in that same turn:\n- A try with a slip you can HEAR (a wrong word or form, a missing   or extra ending) → in at most 10 words name only what to fix   (\"Service, no s. Again.\") and ask them to say it again. Don't   read the whole sentence back, don't react to the content first.\n- A try that doesn't do challenge " +
            (now + 1) +
            " yet (a fragment, or a   different thing) → in at most 15 words say what's missing and   give the example fitted to them, then ask them to say it." +
            weakRule +
            "\n- A clean try right after you asked them to fix it → in at most 8   words: that was right, once more so it sticks.\n- A clean try otherwise → a short, real \"you got it\", then move on   to the next challenge: a natural question that asks for it, and   how they could say it (its example, fitted to them). After the   last one, start the FINAL CHECK: ask them to say it all in their   own words, in one go — no example, no list of the parts, no hints.\n- Not a try (they're talking with you, asking something, asking   for help, or said it in their own language) → answer it as their   conversation partner and help. No verdict.\nJudge only what you can hear (see NEVER TEACH WHAT THE TRANSCRIBER DID below); when you're not sure it's a slip, it isn't. The app checks every try a moment after you answer and keeps the checklist: when it found something you didn't, a note in square brackets tells you — take it back in a few words in your next line."

    /** iOS `tutorBlock`'s rule for a goal's lesson: the real situation's bar. */
    fun tutorWeakRule(goal: String): String =
        "\n- A clean try that the person on the other side of the real   situation (" +
            goal +
            ") would hold against them — generic (\"I love   Lufthansa\", \"it's my dream\"), a claim with no reason or example,   something any candidate could say → in at most 25 words say how   that person would hear it and one way to make it stronger from   their own life, and ask them to try again. The same bar at every   level: that person won't lower it."

    /** iOS `tutorBlock`'s lesson part while a lesson is being planned;
